{"purpose":"Define what happens to a message whose recipient is not connected — the capability the tailnet does not provide and which walkie therefore has to own.","content":"## Requirement\n\nA message addressed to a device that is offline must be retained by the coordinator and delivered when that device reconnects, and the user must be told on delivery.\n\n## Behaviour\n\n- **Delivery is at-least-once on the wire, deduplicated at the receiver** by message ULID. Exactly-once delivery is not attempted at the transport layer; it is achieved at the application layer by making redelivery harmless.\n- **Resumption is by position, not by replay of everything.** The receiver reports the last position it has acknowledged; the coordinator sends what follows.\n- **A message typed while the sender is offline is not lost.** The sender holds it locally and transmits on reconnect.\n- **Retention is bounded.** Both a time-to-live and a size cap are required. An unbounded queue is not a feature; it is a disk-exhaustion path on a staging server.\n\n## Security consequence\n\nA message parked on the coordinator's disk has left the tailnet's transport protection. Queued payloads are therefore in scope for application-level encryption — see the security model decision.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. A message sent to an offline device is delivered when that device reconnects.\n2. A device reconnecting twice in a row receives each pending message once, not twice.\n3. A message composed while the sender is offline is delivered after the sender reconnects.\n4. Messages exceeding the retention TTL are removed, and the removal is observable in logs.\n5. The coordinator refuses to grow the queue past its configured size cap, and reports the refusal rather than failing silently."}